{"concepts_used":["GC-0121"],"decline_reason":"","declined":false,"citations":[{"ref":"TMM/Part14/x-annex-a5---using-the-trade-marks-classification-search/search-results-screen-keyword-search","quote":"Searching for CAR returns CAR and CARS but not CARPET, CARRIAGE, CARBON etc"},{"ref":"TMM/Part14/x-annex-a5---using-the-trade-marks-classification-search/keyword-search-part-word-search","quote":"Can conduct part word keyword search using the asterisk"},{"ref":"TMM/Part14/x-annex-a5---using-the-trade-marks-classification-search/keyword-search-part-word-search","quote":"Searching for CAR* returns CAR and CARS and CARPET, CARRIAGE, CARBON etc"},{"ref":"TMM/Part14/x-annex-a5---using-the-trade-marks-classification-search/keyword-search-part-word-search","quote":"Searching for *CAR* would return CAR, CARS, CARPET, HYDROCARBON, MASCARA, CARE, PLACARD etc"}],"answer":"Manual practice: the Classification Search distinguishes an ordinary keyword search from a part-word search using an asterisk. Its stated behaviour is that CAR returns CAR and CARS, but not CARPET, CARRIAGE or CARBON [1–2].

To include longer words beginning with CAR, search for CAR*: the Manual lists CARPET among the results [3]. To find CAR within words as well, use *CAR*; the examples include HYDROCARBON and MASCARA [4].

The passages document this search behaviour but do not explain the underlying technical mechanism for including CARS in a CAR search. This is Manual guidance, not an Act or Regulations rule."}
